          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "A combination code is a single code that holds two conditions, or a condition and its complication, together. The reference says that where a combination code exists, it is used in place of separate codes. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n\nConditions in these results that use combination codes:\n\n- **Hypertension (coding hypertension, I10 document):** Hypertension alone is I10. When organ involvement is documented, the classification prefers a combination code:\n  - Hypertensive heart disease is coded I11. If heart failure is present, a second code from I50 gives the type.\n  - Hypertension with chronic kidney disease is coded I12, with a second code for the stage of kidney disease.\n  - Both together are coded I13.\n  - Hypertension due to an identifiable underlying cause is secondary hypertension, coded I15 with the cause.\n  - Hypertensive urgency and hypertensive emergency are distinguished within I16.\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11 document):** The E11 family has its own combination codes. Some still need a second code. For example, diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth.\n\nThe reference also explains a general \"with\" convention. When the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says they are unrelated. This is why type 2 diabetes plus chronic kidney disease is coded as diabetic kidney disease by default. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories.)\n\nThe results did not cover every chapter of the classification, so I cannot say which other conditions use combination codes. These passages are coding guidance for clinicians and coders, not advice for an individual's care. If you have a specific diagnosis or code on a bill or record, ask the clinician or the billing office to explain it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
